NAME
       gzip, gunzip, zcat - compress or expand files

SYNOPSIS
       gzip [OPTION]... [FILE]...
       gunzip [OPTION]... [FILE]...
       zcat [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       gzip compresses each FILE and replaces it with FILE.gz, keeping the
       owner, permissions and times. gunzip (the same as gzip -d) turns
       FILE.gz back into FILE. zcat (the same as gzip -dc) writes the
       expanded contents to standard output and leaves the file alone. With
       no FILE, or when FILE is -, gzip reads standard input and writes
       standard output.

       gzip works on single files only. To pack a directory, use tar with
       -z (tar czf backup.tar.gz DIR), which makes a .tar.gz (or .tgz)
       archive. bzip2 (.bz2) and xz (.xz) work the same way with different
       compression.

OPTIONS
       -c, --stdout
              Write to standard output and keep the original file.

       -d, --decompress
              Expand instead of compress.

       -k, --keep
              Keep the original file next to the new one.

       -f, --force
              Overwrite an existing output file, and compress links too.

       -l, --list
              For .gz files: show the compressed and original sizes.

       -r, --recursive
              Compress every file in the directories given.

       -t, --test
              Check that a .gz file is intact.

       -v, --verbose
              Show each file's name and how much it shrank.

       -1 ... -9, --fast, --best
              Speed against size: -1 is fastest, -9 is smallest (default -6).

EXAMPLES
              $ gzip report.csv                 (leaves report.csv.gz)
              $ gunzip report.csv.gz            (back to report.csv)
              $ gzip -k /var/tmp/big.log        (keep big.log too)
              $ zcat notes.txt.gz | grep error

SEE ALSO
       tar(1), bzip2(1), xz(1), zip(1)
